%% Chapter 38: LR Parsing: Theory and Practice
\label{ch:lr-parsing-theory}

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:lr-theory-intro}

This chapter delves deeper into the theory and practice of LR parsing, building on the foundations laid in \cref{ch:bottom-up-parsing}. We explore the formal theory of LR(k) parsing, its relationship to deterministic pushdown automata, techniques for compressing parsing tables, error recovery strategies, and the extension to generalized LR parsing for ambiguous grammars.

\section{The Theory of LR(k) Parsing}
\label{sec:lrk-theory}

\subsection{The LR(k) Condition}
\label{sec:lrk-condition}

\begin{definition}[LR(k) Grammar]
\label{def:lrk}
A grammar $G$ is \textbf{LR(k)} if for any two rightmost derivations:
\begin{align*}
  S &\mderives_{\mathrm{rm}} \alpha A w \mderives_{\mathrm{rm}} \alpha \beta w \\
  S &\mderives_{\mathrm{rm}} \gamma B x \mderives_{\mathrm{rm}} \alpha \beta y
\end{align*}
if $\mathrm{first}_k(w) = \mathrm{first}_k(y)$, then $\alpha A = \gamma B$ and $w = y$.

In other words, the handle and the production to reduce by can be determined uniquely from the prefix of the right sentential form up to the end of the handle and the next $k$ input symbols.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[LR(k) Characterization]
\label{thm:lrk-char}
A grammar is LR(k) if and only if the language of viable prefixes with lookahead, $\mathrm{VP}_k(G)$, is a regular language.
\end{theorem}

\subsection{LR(k) = Deterministic CFL}
\label{sec:lrk-dcfl}

\begin{theorem}[LR(k) and DCFL]
\label{thm:lrk-dcfl}
The class of languages generated by LR(k) grammars is exactly the class of deterministic context-free languages (DCFLs).
\end{theorem}

This fundamental result, due to Knuth (1965), establishes that LR parsing is the most powerful deterministic parsing technique: no deterministic parser can recognize a language that is not LR(k) for some $k$.

\begin{corollary}[LR(k) Hierarchy]
\label{cor:lrk-hierarchy}
$\mathrm{LR}(0) \subsetneq \mathrm{SLR}(1) \subseteq \mathrm{LALR}(1) \subsetneq \mathrm{LR}(1) \subsetneq \mathrm{LR}(2) \subsetneq \cdots$

All of these classes generate the same class of languages (the DCFLs), but each class accepts a strictly larger class of grammars.
\end{corollary}

\section{Relationship to Deterministic Pushdown Automata}
\label{sec:lr-dpda}

\subsection{DPDA and LR Parsers}
\label{sec:dpda-lr}

An LR parser is essentially a deterministic pushdown automaton (DPDA) where:
\begin{itemize}
  \item The stack contains the grammar symbols seen so far (interleaved with states)
  \item The finite control is the parsing table
  \item The transitions are shift and reduce actions
\end{itemize}

\begin{theorem}[LR Parser as DPDA]
\label{thm:lr-dpda}
For every LR(k) grammar $G$, the LR(k) parser for $G$ is a DPDA that recognizes $L(G)$ by empty stack.
\end{theorem}

\subsection{From DPDA to LR Grammar}
\label{sec:dpda-to-lr}

The converse is also true: for every DPDA, there exists an equivalent LR(1) grammar. This establishes the tight correspondence between the automata-theoretic and grammar-theoretic views of deterministic parsing.

\section{Parser Table Compression}
\label{sec:table-compression}

\subsection{The Size Problem}
\label{sec:size-problem}

LR parsing tables can be large. A canonical LR(1) table for a typical programming language grammar may have hundreds of states and dozens of terminals, resulting in a table with tens of thousands of entries. Efficient storage and lookup are essential for practical implementations.

\subsection{Default Values}
\label{sec:default-values}

The simplest compression technique uses \emph{default values}:
\begin{itemize}
  \item For each state, identify the most common action (usually a reduce action)
  \item Store only the exceptions to the default
  \item Use a separate array for default actions
\end{itemize}

This can reduce table size by 50--80\% for typical grammars.

\subsection{Row Compression}
\label{sec:row-compression}

Many states in the ACTION table have similar rows. \emph{Row compression} groups similar rows and uses indirection:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Duplicate row elimination}: If two states have identical ACTION rows, share a single copy.
  \item \textbf{Similar row merging}: Group rows that differ in only a few entries.
  \item \textbf{Compressed sparse row}: Store only non-default entries, using a compact encoding.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Column Compression}
\label{sec:column-compression}

\emph{Column compression} exploits the fact that many terminals behave similarly:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Terminal grouping}: Group terminals that always trigger the same action.
  \item \textbf{Default-to-error}: Use error as the default ACTION entry.
  \item \textbf{Range encoding}: Encode sets of terminals as ranges.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Vector Compression}
\label{sec:vector-compression}

Advanced techniques compress the table as a whole:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Compressed table lookup} (Aho and Ullman): Use a single vector with base and offset arrays.
  \item \textbf{List-based compression}: Store non-default entries in linked lists.
  \item \textbf{Hash-based compression}: Use hash tables for sparse entries.
\end{itemize}

\begin{algorithm}[htbp]
\caption{Look up $\texttt{ACTION}[\texttt{state}, \texttt{symbol}]$}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\State $\texttt{base} \gets \texttt{BASE}[\texttt{state}]$
\State $\texttt{index} \gets \texttt{base} + \texttt{symbol}$
\If{$\texttt{CHECK}[\texttt{index}] = \texttt{state}$}
  \State \textbf{return} $\texttt{ACTION}[\texttt{index}]$
\Else
  \State \textbf{return} $\texttt{DEFAULT}[\texttt{state}]$
\EndIf
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\section{Error Recovery in LR Parsers}
\label{sec:lr-error-recovery}

\subsection{Error Detection}
\label{sec:lr-error-detection}

In an LR parser, an error is detected when the ACTION table entry for the current state and lookahead is blank (no valid action). At this point, the parser must:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Report the error
  \item Recover from the error and continue parsing
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Panic Mode Recovery}
\label{sec:lr-panic}

\emph{Panic mode} recovery discards input symbols until a synchronizing token is found:

\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\State Identify the set of synchronizing tokens for the current state
\State These are typically tokens in the Follow sets of nonterminals on the stack
\State Discard input tokens until a synchronizing token is found
\State Pop stack symbols until a state that can process the synchronizing token is reached
\State Resume parsing
\end{algorithmic}

\subsection{Phrase-Level Recovery}
\label{sec:lr-phrase}

\emph{Phrase-level} recovery modifies the local configuration of the parser:

\begin{itemize}
  \item Insert a token on the input or the stack
  \item Delete a token from the input
  \item Replace a token with another
\end{itemize}

The recovery strategy should minimize the number of changes and avoid cascading errors.

\subsection{Error Productions}
\label{sec:error-productions}

\emph{Error productions} are grammar rules that explicitly handle common errors:

\begin{grammar}{Error Productions}
  stmt & \longrightarrow \textbf{error} ; \mid \textbf{error} \}
\end{grammar}

When the parser encounters an error, it can shift the special \texttt{error} token and then look for a synchronizing token (e.g., \texttt{;} or \texttt{\}}).

\subsection{Automatic Error Recovery}
\label{sec:lr-auto-recovery}

Modern LR parser generators provide automatic error recovery:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Bison}: Provides \texttt{\%error-token} and automatic panic mode recovery
  \item \textbf{Menhir}: Provides automatic error recovery with detailed error messages
  \item \textbf{ANTLR}: Provides automatic error recovery for LL parsing
\end{itemize}

\section{Generalized LR Parsing}
\label{sec:glr}

\subsection{Motivation}
\label{sec:glr-motivation}

Standard LR parsing fails on ambiguous grammars and some unambiguous non-LR grammars. \emph{Generalized LR} (GLR) parsing extends LR parsing to handle these cases by exploring multiple parse paths simultaneously.

\subsection{The GLR Algorithm}
\label{sec:glr-algorithm}

The GLR algorithm, developed by Masaru Tomita (1984), extends the LR parser with two key mechanisms:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Node splitting}: When a shift--reduce or reduce--reduce conflict is encountered, the parser splits into multiple parallel parse threads.
  \item \textbf{Graph-structured stack}: Instead of a single stack, the parser uses a graph-structured stack (GSS) that allows multiple parse threads to share common prefixes.
\end{itemize}

\begin{algorithm}[htbp]
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\State In state $s$ with lookahead $a$:
\If{ACTION[$s$, $a$] has multiple entries}
  \For{each action $a_i$ in ACTION[$s$, $a$]}
    \State Create a new parse thread with action $a_i$
    \State Fork the graph-structured stack
  \EndFor
\EndIf
\State Continue parsing all threads in parallel
\State Merge threads that reach the same state with the same stack
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Graph-Structured Stack}
\label{sec:gss}

The \emph{graph-structured stack} (GSS) is a directed acyclic graph where:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Each node represents a parser state and a grammar symbol
  \item Edges represent stack links (parent--child relationships)
  \item Multiple parse threads share nodes when their stacks have common prefixes
\end{itemize}

\begin{definition}[Graph-Structured Stack]
\label{def:gss}
A \textbf{graph-structured stack} is a DAG where each node $n$ has:
\begin{itemize}
  \item A state label $\texttt{state}(n)$
  \item A symbol label $\texttt{symbol}(n)$
  \item A set of parent pointers $\texttt{parents}(n)$
\end{itemize}
A ``stack'' is a path from a top node to the bottom node.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Complexity of GLR Parsing}
\label{sec:glr-complexity}

\begin{theorem}[GLR Complexity]
\label{thm:glr-complexity}
GLR parsing runs in:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $O(n)$ time and space for unambiguous inputs (same as LR)
  \item $O(n^3)$ time and $O(n^2)$ space in the worst case (for highly ambiguous inputs)
\end{itemize}
where $n$ is the length of the input.
\end{theorem}

\section{Practical Considerations and Optimizations}
\label{sec:lr-practical}

\subsection{Table-Driven vs.\ Direct-Coded}
\label{sec:table-vs-direct}

LR parsers can be implemented in two ways:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Table-driven}: The parser reads actions from a table. Flexible but slower due to table lookup overhead.
  \item \textbf{Direct-coded}: The parser is generated as a set of functions or goto-based code. Faster but less flexible.
\end{itemize}

Direct-coded parsers can be 2--5 times faster than table-driven parsers for typical grammars.

\subsection{Incremental Parsing}
\label{sec:incremental}

For IDEs and interactive environments, \emph{incremental parsing} reuses parse information from previous versions of the source file. When the user edits the source, only the affected portion of the parse tree is re-parsed.

\subsection{Parallel Parsing}
\label{sec:parallel}

LR parsing can be parallelized by:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Partitioning the input into segments and parsing each segment independently
  \item Using speculative parsing to explore multiple parse paths in parallel
  \item Exploiting the structure of the grammar to identify independent sub-parses
\end{itemize}

\section{LR Parsing Implementation Details}
\label{sec:lr-implementation}

\subsection{The LR Parsing Driver}
\label{sec:lr-driver}

The LR parsing driver is a simple loop that reads the parsing tables and executes actions:

\begin{algorithm}[htbp]
\caption{Drive LR parser with tables ACTION and GOTO}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\State Push state 0 onto the stack
\State $\texttt{ip} \gets 0$ \Comment{input pointer}
\Loop
  \State $s \gets$ top of stack
  \State $a \gets \texttt{input}[\texttt{ip}]$
  \State $\texttt{action} \gets \texttt{ACTION}[s, a]$
  \Switch{action}
    \Case{$\texttt{shift } s'$}
      \State Push $a$ and $s'$ onto the stack
      \State $\texttt{ip} \gets \texttt{ip} + 1$
    \EndCase
    \Case{$\texttt{reduce } A \to \beta$}
      \State Pop $|\beta|$ symbols and states from the stack
      \State $s' \gets$ top of stack
      \State Push $A$ and $\texttt{GOTO}[s', A]$ onto the stack
      \State Execute semantic action for $A \to \beta$
    \EndCase
    \Case{$\texttt{accept}$}
      \State \textbf{return} success
    \EndCase
    \Case{$\texttt{error}$}
      \State Call error recovery routine
    \EndCase
  \EndSwitch
\EndLoop
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Semantic Actions in LR Parsers}
\label{sec:lr-semantic-actions}

In a practical LR parser, semantic actions are associated with productions and executed when reductions are performed. The parser generator translates the grammar specification into parsing tables with embedded actions:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Positioned actions}: Actions can be placed at any point in a production, not just at the end. This is implemented by inserting ``marker nonterminals'' into the grammar.
  \item \textbf{Value stack}: Alongside the state stack, a value stack holds the semantic values associated with grammar symbols.
  \item \textbf{Action execution}: When a reduction occurs, the semantic values of the right-hand side symbols are available, and the result is pushed onto the value stack.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Stack Management}
\label{sec:stack-management}

The LR parser maintains two synchronized stacks:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{State stack}: Contains the parser states (integers indexing into the GOTO table)
  \item \textbf{Value stack}: Contains the semantic values associated with grammar symbols
\end{itemize}

When a shift occurs, both the state and the value are pushed. When a reduce occurs, $|\beta|$ entries are popped and the new nonterminal's value is pushed.

\subsection{Direct-Coded LR Parsers}
\label{sec:direct-coded-lr}

A direct-coded LR parser eliminates the table lookup overhead by generating the parsing logic directly as code:

\begin{verbatim}
  state_0:
    switch (lookahead) {
      case ID: goto state_3;
      case NUM: goto state_4;
      case LPAREN: goto state_5;
      default: goto error_handler;
    }
  state_3:  /* reduce F -> id */
    value = make_id(token_value);
    goto reduce_F_id;
  reduce_F_id:
    /* GOTO[F] from current state */
    switch (stack[top]) {
      case 0: goto state_6;
      case 2: goto state_8;
    }
\end{verbatim}

Direct-coded parsers can be 2--5 times faster than table-driven parsers because they eliminate array indirection and enable compiler optimizations.

\section{Advanced LR Parsing Topics}
\label{sec:advanced-lr}

\subsection{LR Regular Grammars}
\label{sec:lr-regular}

An \emph{LR regular} grammar is one where the lookahead needed for parsing can be described by a regular expression rather than a fixed number of tokens. This extends the power of LR parsing while maintaining efficiency.

\subsection{Bridging LR and LL}
\label{sec:lr-ll-bridge}

There is a deep connection between LR and LL parsing:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Every LR(k) grammar can be transformed into an LL(k') grammar (for some $k'$) by grammar transformation
  \item The LR items correspond to the states of the deterministic pushdown automaton
  \item The LL parsing table can be derived from the LR automaton by a suitable projection
\end{itemize}

\subsection{LR Parsing for Non-Context-Free Languages}
\label{sec:lr-beyond-cfg}

While LR parsing is designed for context-free languages, practical extensions handle some non-context-free features:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Indentation-sensitive languages}: Python-style languages can be handled by adding a preprocessing phase that generates INDENT/DEDENT tokens.
  \item \textbf{Off-side rule}: The lexer tracks indentation levels and inserts synthetic tokens.
  \item \textbf{Context-sensitive keywords}: The lexer maintains state to distinguish keywords from identifiers in context-dependent ways.
\end{itemize}

\section{Performance Benchmarking}
\label{sec:lr-benchmarking}

\subsection{Measuring Parser Performance}
\label{sec:parser-perf}

When evaluating LR parser implementations, several metrics are important:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Parse speed}: Tokens per second or lines per second
  \item \textbf{Memory usage}: Size of parsing tables and runtime memory
  \item \textbf{Table generation time}: Time to construct the parsing tables from the grammar
  \item \textbf{Error recovery quality}: How well the parser recovers from syntax errors
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Optimization Techniques}
\label{sec:lr-opt-techniques}

Advanced optimization techniques for LR parsers include:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{State merging}: Merge states that have identical behavior for all inputs
  \item \textbf{Transition compression}: Use default transitions and range encoding
  \item \textbf{Inline caching}: Cache frequently-used transitions in the parser state
  \item \textbf{JIT compilation}: Generate optimized machine code for the parsing tables at runtime
\end{itemize}

\section{Summary}
\label{sec:lr-theory-summary}

LR parsing is the most powerful and widely used parsing technique for programming languages. This chapter has explored the deeper theoretical and practical aspects of LR parsing:

\begin{itemize}
  \item The formal theory of LR(k) parsing and its characterization of DCFLs
  \item The relationship between LR parsers and deterministic pushdown automata
  \item Table compression techniques for practical LR parser implementations
  \item Error recovery strategies for handling syntax errors
  \item Generalized LR parsing for ambiguous and non-LR grammars
  \item Practical optimizations including direct coding and incremental parsing
\end{itemize}

\begin{historical}
The theory and practice of LR parsing have evolved continuously since Knuth's foundational work. Key milestones include: Knuth's LR(k) theory (1965); DeRemer's LALR(1) (1969); Johnson's Yacc (1975); Tomita's GLR algorithm (1984); Rekers' GLR implementation (1992); Bison's GLR support (2000s); and modern work on incremental and parallel LR parsing for IDE applications. LR parsing remains the gold standard for compiler front ends.
\end{historical}
